.TH "INVOX-HELP-ENVIRONMENT" 1 "" "invox" "invox manual"
.SH NAME
invox-help-environment \- environment variables, default directories, and precedence
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Environment variables and default directories.

Usage:
  invox help environment

Environment variables:
  INVOX_CONFIG_DIR
      Config directory to use in place of the default one, on every OS. invox
      reads config.yaml and the global support files there, and `init` writes
      there. It must exist. --config still wins for config.yaml.
  XDG_CONFIG_HOME
      Base directory for the config directory, on every OS.
      Default: $HOME/.config. A relative value is ignored.
  XDG_DATA_HOME
      Base directory for the default archive directory on Linux, macOS and other
      Unix systems. Default: $HOME/.local/share, or $HOME/Library/Application
      Support on macOS. Not used on Windows. A relative value is ignored.
  APPDATA
      Base directory for the default archive directory on Windows.
      Default: %USERPROFILE%\eAppData\eRoaming. Not used elsewhere.
      A relative value is ignored.
  VISUAL
      Editor for `config`, `customer edit` and `new -e`. Wins over EDITOR.
      The value is split into words like a shell would and run directly, with the
      file as the last argument. A value with shell syntax ($, |, ; and the like)
      runs through sh -c, where an unquoted # starts a comment that drops the file.
      On Windows it is always split and run directly.
  EDITOR
      Editor used when VISUAL is unset, read the same way. Without either: vi, or
      notepad on Windows.
  INVOX_PROMPT_DISABLED
      Any non-empty value works like --no-input. With either, invox never prompts or
      opens an editor, and a step that needs one fails with exit 2.
  INVOX_FORCE_TTY
      Testing aid: any non-empty value makes invox treat stdout as a terminal.

Config directory (config.yaml and the global support files):
  Linux:     $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/invox, else $HOME/.config/invox
  macOS:     $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/invox, else $HOME/.config/invox
  Windows:   %XDG_CONFIG_HOME%\einvox, else %USERPROFILE%\e.config\einvox
  INVOX_CONFIG_DIR replaces it on every OS.
  here:      $HOME/.config/invox

Default archive directory (when config.yaml sets no archive.dir):
  Linux:     $XDG_DATA_HOME/invox/invoices, else $HOME/.local/share/invox/invoices
  macOS:     $XDG_DATA_HOME/invox/invoices, else $HOME/Library/Application Support/invox/invoices
  Windows:   %APPDATA%\einvox\einvoices, else %USERPROFILE%\eAppData\eRoaming\einvox\einvoices
  here:      $HOME/.local/share/invox/invoices

Config file:
  1. --config PATH
  2. config.yaml in INVOX_CONFIG_DIR
  3. config.yaml in the config directory
  A --config file that is missing or broken is an error. invox never falls
  back to another config file.

Precedence:
  An explicit flag wins, then config.yaml, then the defaults above. Environment
  variables only move the default directories; no variable overrides a flag or
  a config.yaml setting.

Support file resolution (customers.yaml, issuer.yaml, invoice_defaults.yaml, template):
  1. explicit flag (-c, -u, --defaults, -t)
  2. upward search from the current directory
  3. paths.* in config.yaml, relative to config.yaml
  4. the file in the config directory

Upward search:
  It always searches the current directory. It goes up to the nearest directory
  that holds .git, invox.yaml or invoice_defaults.yaml, and stops below your
  home directory, which it searches only when invox runs there. Outside your
  home directory, without such a marker above, it searches only the current
  directory.

Archive directory resolution:
  1. archive.dir in config.yaml, relative to config.yaml
  2. the default archive directory above

Run `invox config paths` to see what each lookup finds.

See also:
  invox help config
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBinvox\fP(1)
